; Decision 7's structural bypass audit, wired into `dune test` (fix-wave round 2, re-review finding).
;
; `scripts/check-authorization-checkpoint` proves the claim both the design spec and
; `batch_commit.mli` present as THE proof that no write can reach the replicated log without passing
; the authorization checkpoint. Until this rule existed, nothing ran it automatically: this repo has
; no CI beyond the task-status workflow, so `dune test` is its only automatic gate, and a second
; `Riptide_vsr.Replica.propose` caller introduced in `lib/` tomorrow would have passed `dune build`
; and `dune test` cleanly while both documents went on claiming otherwise. A proof nothing runs is
; prose.
;
; Two details worth knowing rather than rediscovering:
;   - The action runs with `_build/default` as its working directory, which is exactly why the script
;     needs (and has) a filesystem-walk fallback for source discovery -- the sources are copied in
;     there, but `.git` is not, so `git ls-files` cannot work. Running it from the repo root by hand
;     (`scripts/check-authorization-checkpoint`) uses git instead; the script reports which mode it
;     used, so the two are never confused.
;   - `(glob_files_rec lib/*.ml)` is the dependency, not just the script: without it, dune would
;     happily cache a passing result across a later edit to a `lib/` source file, which is the one
;     thing this check must never do.
;
; `scripts/check-citations` is deliberately NOT wired in the same way: it is a whole-repo
; documentation-hygiene aid with a disclosed, nonzero baseline of known warnings (see its own
; docstring), not a binary safety invariant, and it reads `git blame` for its `--drifted` mode, which
; a build sandbox has no repository for.
(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (deps
  scripts/check-authorization-checkpoint
  (glob_files_rec lib/*.ml))
 (action
  (run python3 %{dep:scripts/check-authorization-checkpoint} lib)))
